<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I'm Sleepy. It's Bedtime. – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Evening Routine</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm Sleepy. It's Bedtime.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Explain keywords from readings in simpler terms. Concept: Explain simple events in story form.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain keywords about bedtime routines using gestures (e.g., mimes sleeping for "sleep"). Can explain simple bedtime events in story form with basic actions (e.g., "First, I brush my teeth." and act it out). Can guide peers in understanding bedtime routines by pointing to visuals (e.g., points to bed and says, "Sleep here.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Goodnight Song" or a short "Night-time Mime Game." Students mime actions related to bedtime routines (e.g., brush teeth, read book, sleep) as the teacher calls out the actions. This activates prior knowledge and vocabulary (brush, read, sleep, yawn).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a "bed" and models explaining it using gestures and simpler words. "This is a bed. You sleep here. (Mimes sleeping). It is comfortable." Then, the teacher models explaining "sleepy" by yawning and rubbing eyes, saying, "I am sleepy. My eyes are tired. (Points to eyes)." The teacher also models polite phrases: "Goodnight, sleep well!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you do in a bed? (Sleep!)"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"(Teacher yawns) Am I happy or sleepy? (Sleepy!)"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Evening Routine."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated story or picture sequence titled "My Bedtime Story" showing a child's evening routine, using sequencing words (First, Then, Next, Last) and target vocabulary (wash face, brush teeth, wear pajamas, read book, go to bed, sleep). Each picture has a simple sentence. For example: "First, I wash my face. Then I brush my teeth. Next, I wear my pajamas. Last, I read my favorite book and go to bed. Goodnight!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Mediating simple information about daily routines by explaining keywords and describing events using basic language and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to pictures. "What is the story about? (Bedtime routine!)" "Is it morning or night? (Night!)"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sequencing/Matching:</b> Students receive mixed-up picture cards from the story. They arrange them in order while listening to the story again. The teacher asks, "What is First?" (Students hold up picture of washing face). "What is Last?" (Students hold up picture of sleeping).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice explaining keywords and simple events using sequencing words (First, then, next, last), present simple for daily routines (I brush my teeth), time and status expressions (It's bedtime, I am sleepy), and target vocabulary (bed, pajamas, brush, wash, sleep, story). Pronunciation of /b/ in "bed" and /t/ in "teeth", blending /w-ɒ-ʃ/ in "wash" and /t-iː-θ/ in "teeth".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Keyword Explanation Chain (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher writes target vocabulary words (e.g., 'sleep', 'brush', 'pajamas', 'bed') on the board. The teacher points to a word, explains it with actions/simple words, and then asks a student to explain another word. Example: Teacher: "Sleep. (Mimes sleeping). You close your eyes." Student 1: "Brush. (Mimes brushing teeth). Clean teeth." Student 2: "Pajamas. (Points to pajamas). Clothes for sleep." This reinforces explaining in simpler terms.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Routine Sequence Sentence Starters (Semi-Controlled):</b> Students work in pairs with picture cards of evening routine activities. The teacher provides sentence frames: "First, I _____. Then, I _____." or "It's bedtime. I am sleepy. I _____. Goodnight!" Students take turns saying a sequence of 2-3 steps and explaining each step with a simple action or word (e.g., "First, I wash my face." (Mimes washing). "Then, I brush my teeth." (Mimes brushing).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Explain It to Your Peer" (Pair Interaction):</b> Students are given a small list of 3-4 vocabulary words (e.g., 'pillow', 'blanket', 'yawn', 'dark'). In pairs, one student picks a word and tries to explain it to their partner using only gestures and very simple words (like a mini charades game). The partner guesses. They switch roles. This directly practices mediating meaning.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Evening Routine." Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion, specifically for the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Project 2: Evening Role-Play - Mediating the Routine.</b> Students work in pairs. One student (the "explainer") has a sequence of 3-4 picture cards depicting an evening routine (e.g., brush teeth, wear pajamas, read book, go to bed). The other student (the "listener") has blank cards or a simple outline. The "explainer" must guide their partner through the routine using sequencing words, gestures, and simple explanations for each step, and polite phrases (e.g., "First, I brush my teeth. (Mimes brushing). This makes my teeth clean. Can you show me 'brush'?" or "Next, I put on my pajamas. (Points to pajamas). These are clothes for sleep." "Goodnight, sleep well!"). The "listener" draws or points to the corresponding action based on the explanation. This is a mediation task where students guide their peers to understand and recreate information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher's Role:</b> Circulate, provide support, prompt with questions like "What does 'sleepy' mean?" or "What comes next?", and encourage peer interaction and explanation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Teacher observes pairs during the "Evening Role-Play - Mediating the Routine" activity. Students perform their role-play, focusing on explaining the routine and clarifying keywords for their partner.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Explanation:</b> Can explain keywords using gestures/simpler terms.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Sequencing Words:</b> Uses "First, then, next, last" correctly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates vocabulary related to bedtime (e.g., bed, pajamas, brush, story).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic Competence:</b> Uses "Goodnight" and polite requests (e.g., "Can you read me a bedtime story, please?").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of /b/ and /t/, and blends /w-ɒ-ʃ/ and /t-iː-θ/.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Actively guides and helps peer understand.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple worksheet with two prompts:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can explain 'sleep' by ________ (draw or write a word)."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can tell my friend 'First, I _____. Then, I _____.'" (Draw two actions)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to explain to my friend today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new way I can explain a word like 'sleepy'?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The mediation activities were very engaging, especially the "Explain It to Your Peer" game and the role-play. Students were able to use gestures and simple words effectively to clarify meaning for their partners. Some students needed more prompting for sequencing words, and I noticed a few struggled with explaining abstract concepts like "dream." For future lessons, I will provide more explicit sentence frames for explanations or use more concrete visual aids for less tangible vocabulary. The pronunciation focus on /b/ and /t/ and blending was well integrated, though continuous reinforcement is necessary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "I'm Sleepy. It's Bedtime." theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme and set of vocabulary, building on the communicative skills developed in this unit, potentially focusing on a different daily routine or activity.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>